proof_profiler_delta (per-command proof-state-size profiler) — implementation plan
===================================================================================

status: planned (2026-08-13)
NOT scheduled into a wave. one of three independent, parallel profiler
experiments (time / memory / proof-size-delta) built in separate
worktrees; this plan covers proof-size-delta only. spec:
D-2026-08-13-proof-profiler-delta.

the name
--------

`profile_proof_delta`, not `proof_profiler` — the tool measures one
specific thing (goal-state SIZE delta per command), and the name says
what it reports rather than what family it belongs to. leaves room for
sibling tools (`profile_proof_time`, `profile_proof_memory`) named the
same way, each its own experiment.

scala or ML?
------------

ML ONLY, same reasoning as plans/proof_tasks: this is a prover-side
operation (parse text, run transitions, inspect a `Proof.state`'s
goal) with no scala analogue, and `mcp_tool` registration gets
`tools/list` for free. A new theory `MCP_Profile_Delta.thy` in the
`MCP-HOL` tree (next to `MCP_Repl.thy`), importing `MCP-HOL.MCP` (the
HOL-anchored entry point — never `MCP-Tools.MCP_Tools` directly, see
`MCP.thy`'s own doc comment for the merge-order footgun), one
`mcp_tool profile_proof_delta` declaration, ML structure
`MCP_Profile_Delta`.

why not `ir/ir.ML`/`MCP_Repl.repl_tab`: the brief and the spec entry
are explicit — this tool must not mutate any live repl. It also has a
different shape of input (a THEORY plus a whole batch of TEXT, not a
single step against a named, already-claimed repl), so reusing
`Ir.step`'s claim/release machinery would be the wrong abstraction
even if mutation were acceptable. Instead it builds its OWN private,
disposable `Toplevel.state`, the same construction `Ir.init`'s
`from_specs` uses (`ir/ir.ML:439-448`) but written fresh here — new
code, not an edit to `ir/ir.ML` (verbatim-reused symlink, MIT header
constraint shared with other in-flight work).

input contract
--------------

  profile_proof_delta {theory: "HOL.Main", isar_text: "lemma ... qed"}
    -> profiling report TEXT

NAMING DEVIATION FROM THE BRIEF, found empirically while building this
(trust the code over the brief, per the ground rules): the brief's
input contract said `text`, but bare `text` is itself a registered
Isar COMMAND keyword, so `Outer_Syntax` splits the `mcp_tool`
declaration's own span right at that token -- "name expected, but
end-of-input was found". Renamed to `isar_text`, which also matches
`repl_step`'s own parameter name for the same kind of argument.

`theory` resolved via `Thy_Info.get_theory` (idiom `MCP_Repl.entities`
already uses, `MCP_Repl.thy:101`) — the theory must already be
heap-resident (built into the session the MCP server loaded), same
precondition `Ir.init` documents for its own specs. `isar_text` is Isar
source, typed `source` (not `string`) in the `params` clause — it will
routinely contain embedded newlines (a multi-command proof script),
and `check_value` (`MCP_Tools.thy:681-686`) rejects newlines in a
`string`-typed argument outright.

what a report line carries
---------------------------

  name       Toplevel.name_of tr — the command keyword ("lemma",
             "apply", "qed", ...)
  position   Toplevel.pos_of tr, formatted as a line number
             (Position.line_of, the same idiom MCP_Repl.entities uses)
  nprems_delta / size_delta
             int, or the literal "N/A" — see "the N/A rule" below

the N/A rule
------------

Before and after each transition, the toplevel state is measured IFF
`Toplevel.is_proof st` holds:

  Thm.nprems_of goal                          — subgoal count
  size_of_term (Thm.prop_of goal)              — term size (Pure/term.ML)

where `goal` comes from `#goal (Proof.goal (Toplevel.proof_of st))`.
A theory-level state (not mid-proof) measures as ABSENT, not 0 — a
theory-level command ("lemma", "qed", a bare "text" command) did not
"shrink from nothing", it simply is not a proof step. Concretely:

  - a command whose BEFORE state is theory-level and AFTER state is
    mid-proof (`lemma` opening a goal) has no before-measurement to
    diff against: delta = N/A.
  - a command whose BEFORE state is mid-proof and AFTER state is
    theory-level (`qed`, `done` closing the last goal) has no
    after-measurement: delta = N/A. This is deliberate, not a gap —
    see the spec entry's discussion of "the step is absent-from-proof
    after qed": we do not invent a synthetic "0 subgoals" reading for
    a state that has left proof mode; the state genuinely has no goal
    to measure.
  - a command whose BEFORE and AFTER are BOTH mid-proof (the common
    case: `apply`, `by`, a `have`/`show` inside a structured proof)
    reports a real delta = after − before, which can be positive
    (growth), negative (shrink), or zero.
  - a command whose BEFORE and AFTER are BOTH theory-level (anything
    outside any proof — `definition`, `datatype`, a bare `text`)
    reports N/A on both sides.

`Proof.goal` can itself raise (e.g. a proof state with unresolved
obligations elsewhere); the measurement is wrapped in `try`, and a
raised exception during MEASUREMENT (not during the transition itself)
also reads as N/A rather than aborting the whole call — an unusual
proof-state shape should degrade the one number, not the entire
report.

sort order
----------

Report lines split into two groups, in this order:

  1. lines with a REAL delta (both sides measured), sorted by
     nprems_delta descending, ties broken by size_delta descending —
     "largest growth first".
  2. lines with delta = N/A, in original source order — listed (per
     the spec: "less interesting but still listed"), not hidden.

a summary line closes the report: total command count, count with a
real delta, count that grew (nprems_delta > 0 or size_delta > 0), N/A
count.

error handling: WHOLE-CALL, not partial results
------------------------------------------------

`Outer_Syntax.parse_text` + a `List.foldl` over
`Toplevel.command_exception false tr st` — the same shape as
`ir/ir.ML`'s `exec_text` (`ir/ir.ML:369-384`) — means a transition that
raises propagates out of the fold immediately; nothing catches it here
either. So a syntax error or a failing tactic anywhere in `isar_text` fails
the WHOLE `profile_proof_delta` call, with no partial report for the
commands that ran before the failure.

This is a real design choice, not an oversight, and it deliberately
matches `repl_step`'s documented failure semantics ("if a step FAILS,
the REPL state is UNCHANGED") rather than inventing a second
convention: this tool's job is to profile text that already WORKS
(text an agent has already gotten to replay cleanly, e.g. via
`repl_step`/`repl_replay`), not to triage syntax/tactic failures —
`repl_step` and `check_theory` already own that job, with error
messages tuned for it. A future iteration could offer partial results
as an opt-in mode; not built here (see ASSUMPTIONS A3 below for the
test that pins the current behavior).

implementation steps
---------------------

1. `MCP_Profile_Delta.thy` skeleton: imports `MCP-HOL.MCP`, ML
   structure with a `measure : Toplevel.state -> (int * int) option`
   function (`Thm.nprems_of` / `size_of_term`, guarded by
   `Toplevel.is_proof` + `try`). [ml unit: measure on a hand-built
   mid-proof state vs a theory-level state]
2. state construction: `build_state : string -> Toplevel.state`,
   `Thy_Info.get_theory` + `Theory.begin_theory` + `Toplevel.make_state`.
   [ml unit: builds without error for a heap-resident theory name;
   errors clearly for an unknown theory name]
3. the profiling fold: parse `isar_text` into transitions, fold
   `Toplevel.command_exception false tr st`, measuring before/after
   each transition, collecting `{name, pos, before, after}` entries.
   [ml unit: entry count matches transition count for a short
   multi-step proof]
4. delta computation + N/A rule (mechanism above). [ml unit: entry
   whose before/after are both mid-proof has a real int delta; entry
   crossing the theory/proof boundary (lemma, qed) has delta = N/A]
5. sort + format + summary line. [ml unit: golden text for a small
   fixture proof with a known growth step]
6. `mcp_tool profile_proof_delta` declaration (`run` form: full
   context + args, params `theory :: string`, `isar_text :: source`,
   annotations `read_only`). [ml unit: tool is registered, callable
   through `MCP_Tool`'s own run path]
7. rebuild (`tools/wt-isabelle-build.sh <name> build`), confirm
   MCP-HOL / MCP-HOL-Tests green.
8. AFP demo (see brief) — not a test, a manual capture of real output
   against 2-3 AFP entries.

ASSUMPTIONS -> tests
--------------------

- A1 GROWTH IS VISIBLE. the whole point of the tool: an `induct` (or
  a case split) that turns 1 subgoal into N, and/or an `unfold` that
  inflates term size, must show up as a large positive delta ranked
  at the top. test: a fixture lemma proved by `(induct xs)` producing
  ≥2 subgoals; assert the induct step's nprems_delta > 0 and it sorts
  above a later `auto`/`simp` step that closes goals. [ml unit,
  MCP_Profile_Delta_Tests.thy]
- A2 SHRINK / CLOSE READS RIGHT. a step that closes the last subgoal
  (nprems_of -> 0 mid-proof, e.g. `apply simp` finishing a proof still
  inside `apply`-style) reports a real NEGATIVE delta, not N/A — only
  the actual theory/proof BOUNDARY crossings (lemma-open, qed/done)
  read as N/A. test: fixture with an `apply`-chain vs a fixture ending
  in `qed`; assert the two read differently (real negative delta vs
  N/A). [ml unit]
- A3 NON-PROOF COMMANDS READ N/A, NEVER 0. test: a `definition`/
  `datatype` command in the middle of `isar_text` (outside any proof)
  reports N/A on both subgoal and size columns. [ml unit]
- A4 WHOLE-CALL FAILURE. an `isar_text` whose last command fails (bad
  tactic) fails the ENTIRE `profile_proof_delta` call — no partial
  report reaches the caller — mirroring `repl_step`'s atomicity.
  test: fixture with 3 good commands then 1 bad one; assert the tool
  call itself errors (not "returns a 3-line report"). [ml unit]
- A5 REPORT SORTING is stable and matches the documented order:
  real-delta lines by nprems_delta desc / size_delta desc, N/A lines
  after in source order. test: fixture mixing a shrink step, a growth
  step, and an N/A (theory-level) step; assert output line order.
  [ml unit]
- A6 READ-ONLY / NO REPL MUTATION. calling `profile_proof_delta` does
  not create, claim, or otherwise touch any entry in `Ir`'s
  `repl_tab`. test: call `repl_list`/`Ir.repls` before and after a
  `profile_proof_delta` call in the same session; assert no new
  entries and no error about a busy repl. [ml unit, exercises
  `Ir.repls` which is the same accessor `repl_list` uses]

done when
---------

- A1..A6 green; `tools/list` shows `profile_proof_delta`; MCP-HOL and
  MCP-HOL-Tests build clean; the AFP demo (see brief) captured against
  2-3 real entries.
